{# One READ-ONLY Billing Detail / Shipping Detail card on a sell-side document's VIEW screen.

   Not to be confused with `sales_address_cards.html.twig`, which is the FORM side: a picker, a
   set of posted inputs and the country/province controls that re-derive one from the other.
   Nothing here posts and nothing here is editable — this card states what the document froze.

   Six copies of it existed, two on each of admin/order/detail, admin/invoice/detail and
   admin/estimate/detail, thirteen rows apiece. They had already drifted in three ways that are
   each a different answer to "what does this document say about where the goods went", and all
   three are parameters here rather than a `{% if document == ... %}`:

     1. WHEN THERE IS NO ADDRESS AT ALL. The order and the quote print the thirteen rows anyway,
        filling four of them from the company snapshot and the rest with dashes. The invoice
        prints a SENTENCE instead, because an invoice raised on its own has nothing to show and
        a grid of empty rows would read as data that failed to load. `emptyMessage` chooses:
        null keeps the rows, a string replaces them.

     2. THE COUNTRY THE ROWS FALL BACK TO. 'Canada' on the order and the quote, a dash on the
        invoice. It reaches the row through `fallbacks.country` like every other fallback, so
        no caller inherits another document's idea of where it ships.

     3. WHAT A BLANK FIELD READS AS WHEN THE ADDRESS EXISTS. This one was invisible and is the
        reason the panel could not simply be lifted: the order and the quote print an EMPTY
        <strong> for a field the snapshot left null, while the invoice prints a dash. On a
        shipping snapshot with no fax and no second e-mail that is four cells that differ
        between two documents describing one shipment. `fieldFallback` states it: '' keeps the
        empty cell, '-' fills it.

   Parameters
   ----------
   address        required  the document's frozen snapshot for this panel, or null. Never an
                            address-book row read live — a document says what it said.
   title          required  the card's heading: 'Billing Detail' or 'Shipping Detail'.
   fallbacks      optional  what each row reads when `address` is NULL, keyed by the same names
                            the rows below use — companyName, firstName, phone, country,
                            emailPrimary are the five any caller currently sets. Every key left
                            out falls back to '-'. Ignored entirely when `emptyMessage` is given,
                            since then no rows are rendered.
   fieldFallback  optional  what a row reads when `address` EXISTS but that field is blank.
                            Defaults to '', which is what the order and the quote print. The
                            invoice passes '-'.
   emptyMessage   optional  a whole-card sentence to print INSTEAD of the rows when `address` is
                            null. Omit it (or pass null) to keep the rows. Plain text — the
                            invoice builds its two-sentence version in the caller, because
                            whether there was an order to inherit from is the INVOICE's question
                            and not something this card could work out.
   deliverable    optional  true on a card that is a delivery destination, which is the only kind
                            that shows a Delivery instructions row, and only when the snapshot
                            carries one. Defaults to false: a billing card never shows it, even
                            for a snapshot that happens to hold one, which is what all three
                            screens did before this partial existed.
   trailingRows   optional  extra {label, value} rows appended after Secondary Email. The quote's
                            shipping card carries one — its Method — which is a field of the
                            DOCUMENT and not of the address, so it is passed in rather than
                            reached for here. #}

{% set fallbackValues = fallbacks|default({}) %}
{% set blank = fieldFallback|default('') %}
{% set message = emptyMessage|default(null) %}

<section class="detail-panel order-detail-panel">
    <h2>{{ title }}</h2>
    {% if address is null and message is not null %}
        <p style="margin:0; font-size:0.83rem; color:#64748b; line-height:1.6;">{{ message }}</p>
    {% else %}
        {# One list, one rule. The rows used to be thirteen hand-written lines per card and six
           cards, which is how one of them quietly started dashing a blank cell and the others
           did not. `suppressWhenBlank` is the Delivery instructions row's own behaviour: it is
           not a cell that can be empty, it is a row that is absent. #}
        {% set rows = [
            {label: 'Company Name',          key: 'companyName'},
            {label: 'First Name',            key: 'firstName'},
            {label: 'Last Name',             key: 'lastName'},
            {label: 'Phone number',          key: 'phone'},
            {label: 'Street Address',        key: 'addressLine1'},
            {label: 'Address 2',             key: 'addressLine2'},
            {label: 'City',                  key: 'city'},
            {label: 'Province',              key: 'province'},
            {label: 'Delivery instructions', key: 'deliveryInstructions', suppressWhenBlank: true},
            {label: 'Country',               key: 'country'},
            {label: 'Postal Code',           key: 'postalCode'},
            {label: 'Fax Number',            key: 'fax'},
            {label: 'Primary Email',         key: 'emailPrimary'},
            {label: 'Secondary Email',       key: 'emailSecondary'},
        ] %}
        {% for row in rows %}
            {% if row.suppressWhenBlank|default(false) %}
                {% if deliverable|default(false) and address is not null and attribute(address, row.key) %}
                    <div class="detail-row"><label>{{ row.label }}:</label><strong>{{ attribute(address, row.key) }}</strong></div>
                {% endif %}
            {% else %}
                <div class="detail-row"><label>{{ row.label }}:</label><strong>{{ address is not null ? attribute(address, row.key)|default(blank) : (fallbackValues[row.key] ?? '-') }}</strong></div>
            {% endif %}
        {% endfor %}
        {% for row in trailingRows|default([]) %}
            <div class="detail-row"><label>{{ row.label }}:</label><strong>{{ row.value }}</strong></div>
        {% endfor %}
    {% endif %}
</section>
